1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,600
Monday.

2
00:00:01,340 --> 00:00:03,080
The school computer lab, after dark.

3
00:00:03,080 --> 00:00:07,140
Kai has hacked nothing, but has borrowed every

4
00:00:07,140 --> 00:00:07,740
single screen.

5
00:00:08,160 --> 00:00:09,740
On every screen, the same thing.

6
00:00:10,120 --> 00:00:11,360
The rock, and its path.

7
00:00:11,840 --> 00:00:12,960
The rock is still coming.

8
00:00:13,580 --> 00:00:14,740
But now you have a word on a

9
00:00:14,740 --> 00:00:15,200
whiteboard.

10
00:00:16,040 --> 00:00:16,140
Nudge.

11
00:00:17,100 --> 00:00:18,360
You can't catch it, Kai says.

12
00:00:18,780 --> 00:00:19,240
Bo looks offended.

13
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:21,400
So what if you had to stop an

14
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:21,920
asteroid?

15
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:22,980
Not in a movie.

16
00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:24,060
The real way.

17
00:00:25,120 --> 00:00:25,740
Think about it.

18
00:00:25,960 --> 00:00:27,580
The rock weighs about three and a half

19
00:00:27,580 --> 00:00:30,580
billion kilograms, and it moves twenty kilometres every

20
00:00:30,580 --> 00:00:31,000
second.

21
00:00:31,660 --> 00:00:33,640
Catching it means stopping all that energy at

22
00:00:33,640 --> 00:00:33,900
once.

23
00:00:34,040 --> 00:00:35,740
About a hundred and seventy megatons.

24
00:00:35,740 --> 00:00:37,100
Bo's arms are strong.

25
00:00:37,300 --> 00:00:39,280
But a hundred and seventy megatons would turn

26
00:00:39,280 --> 00:00:42,660
Bo, the rock, and the town into vapour.

27
00:00:42,660 --> 00:00:43,680
And blowing it up is no better.

28
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:45,640
You'd turn one big rock into lots of

29
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:47,020
smaller rocks, all still coming.

30
00:00:47,420 --> 00:00:49,300
So the trick isn't stopping it.

31
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:50,560
It's changing where it goes.

32
00:00:50,980 --> 00:00:51,800
A tiny bit.

33
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:52,900
Very early.

34
00:00:54,060 --> 00:00:55,000
Here's the magic.

35
00:00:55,580 --> 00:00:58,220
Change a rock's speed by one centimetre per

36
00:00:58,220 --> 00:00:58,580
second.

37
00:00:58,980 --> 00:00:59,940
Much slower than walking.

38
00:01:00,280 --> 00:01:02,280
Do it ten years early, and that tiny

39
00:01:02,280 --> 00:01:04,680
push adds up to thousands of kilometres by

40
00:01:04,680 --> 00:01:05,260
the time it arrives.

41
00:01:05,720 --> 00:01:06,700
Enough to miss the whole planet.

42
00:01:07,300 --> 00:01:09,240
It's like steering a huge ship.

43
00:01:09,560 --> 00:01:11,680
Turn the wheel early, and a tiny turn

44
00:01:11,680 --> 00:01:12,220
is enough.

45
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:15,160
Turn it late, and nothing can save you.

46
00:01:15,680 --> 00:01:17,280
Push it late, and you need a giant

47
00:01:17,280 --> 00:01:17,680
shove.

48
00:01:18,060 --> 00:01:20,560
Push it early, and a nudge is enough.

49
00:01:21,740 --> 00:01:23,540
That's exactly what NASA tested.

50
00:01:24,060 --> 00:01:26,080
They picked an asteroid called Didymos, which has

51
00:01:26,080 --> 00:01:28,280
its own little moon called Dimorphos.

52
00:01:28,280 --> 00:01:29,492
The moon is about a hundred and fifty

53
00:01:29,492 --> 00:01:30,000
metres wide.

54
00:01:30,140 --> 00:01:31,280
The same size as your rock.

55
00:01:32,020 --> 00:01:34,780
In 2022, the DART spacecraft, about the size

56
00:01:34,780 --> 00:01:37,120
of a small car, flew straight into it,

57
00:01:37,120 --> 00:01:39,620
at twenty-two thousand kilometres an hour.

58
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:42,060
NASA hoped to change the moon's orbit by

59
00:01:42,060 --> 00:01:43,540
at least seventy-three seconds.

60
00:01:43,920 --> 00:01:46,420
It changed it by thirty-two minutes.

61
00:01:47,080 --> 00:01:48,040
And here's the surprise.

62
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:50,800
The rubble that blasted off the surface pushed

63
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:52,800
the rock even more, like a rocket engine

64
00:01:52,800 --> 00:01:53,520
made of gravel.

65
00:01:54,260 --> 00:01:56,680
Now a European spacecraft called Hera is on

66
00:01:56,680 --> 00:01:57,740
its way to look at the crash site

67
00:01:57,740 --> 00:01:58,240
up close.

68
00:01:59,380 --> 00:02:01,300
Scientists have a gentler idea too.

69
00:02:01,760 --> 00:02:04,380
A heavy spacecraft parks next to the asteroid.

70
00:02:04,720 --> 00:02:05,700
It never touches it.

71
00:02:06,060 --> 00:02:08,960
Its own gravity slowly tugs the rock off

72
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:09,200
course.

73
00:02:09,780 --> 00:02:11,020
It's called a gravity tractor.

74
00:02:11,600 --> 00:02:13,660
One idea is that a twenty-tonne spacecraft could

75
00:02:13,660 --> 00:02:16,820
move a two-hundred-metre rock, if it had about

76
00:02:16,820 --> 00:02:17,520
twenty years.

77
00:02:17,740 --> 00:02:18,340
It's slow.

78
00:02:18,760 --> 00:02:20,320
But it doesn't need to hit anything.

79
00:02:21,220 --> 00:02:21,900
Ravi likes this one.

80
00:02:22,140 --> 00:02:24,540
It sounds like what he does, just slower.

81
00:02:25,960 --> 00:02:27,340
So you practise.

82
00:02:27,340 --> 00:02:29,520
On the school football pitch, Ravi pushes a

83
00:02:29,520 --> 00:02:30,700
football without touching it.

84
00:02:30,960 --> 00:02:31,500
Not hard.

85
00:02:31,700 --> 00:02:34,580
Just a tiny push, for a long time.

86
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:37,000
The ball rolls the length of the pitch.

87
00:02:37,560 --> 00:02:39,320
Then Ravi tries again with his eyes closed.

88
00:02:39,820 --> 00:02:40,760
Then with a bigger ball.

89
00:02:41,240 --> 00:02:42,580
Then with Bo's motorbike.

90
00:02:42,580 --> 00:02:45,160
Bo gets bored and kicks the football into

91
00:02:45,160 --> 00:02:45,700
the next street.

92
00:02:46,340 --> 00:02:47,620
Kai makes her go and get it.

93
00:02:47,940 --> 00:02:49,100
By the end of the night, Ravi can

94
00:02:49,100 --> 00:02:51,040
keep a gentle push going for as long

95
00:02:51,040 --> 00:02:51,620
as you like.

96
00:02:52,160 --> 00:02:54,680
The problem is, you don't have ten years.

97
00:02:55,140 --> 00:02:56,080
You have three weeks.

98
00:02:56,620 --> 00:02:57,640
That's where you come in.

99
00:02:58,640 --> 00:03:00,100
That night, the five of you lie on

100
00:03:00,100 --> 00:03:01,480
the grass in the middle of the pitch.

101
00:03:02,120 --> 00:03:04,140
Nia holds Ravi's hand, so he doesn't float

102
00:03:04,140 --> 00:03:04,460
away.

103
00:03:05,060 --> 00:03:06,000
Bo holds Nia's.

104
00:03:06,260 --> 00:03:07,160
Kai counts down.

105
00:03:07,160 --> 00:03:09,540
Ravi raises one hand towards the sky.

106
00:03:09,540 --> 00:03:11,820
And you stop time.

107
00:03:12,940 --> 00:03:13,980
The world freezes.

108
00:03:14,380 --> 00:03:15,860
A bird hangs in the air above the

109
00:03:15,860 --> 00:03:16,080
goal.

110
00:03:16,560 --> 00:03:18,180
Kai is stuck halfway through a blink.

111
00:03:18,760 --> 00:03:20,620
But you reach out and touch Ravi's shoulder,

112
00:03:20,620 --> 00:03:22,360
and he can move too.

113
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:24,500
For everyone else, it's one second.

114
00:03:24,960 --> 00:03:27,720
For Ravi, it's a whole hour of gentle

115
00:03:27,720 --> 00:03:28,200
pushing.

116
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:30,840
Like a gravity tractor with headphones.

117
00:03:31,720 --> 00:03:34,020
An hour of pushing, three weeks early.

118
00:03:35,060 --> 00:03:36,000
You hope it's enough.

119
00:03:36,980 --> 00:03:37,760
Time starts again.

120
00:03:38,280 --> 00:03:39,560
The bright dot in the sky doesn't look

121
00:03:39,560 --> 00:03:40,180
any different.

122
00:03:40,760 --> 00:03:41,620
Nobody breathes.

123
00:03:42,320 --> 00:03:43,360
Kai is checking the numbers.

124
00:03:43,980 --> 00:03:45,280
Then Kai smiles.

125
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:46,660
It's going to miss.

126
00:03:47,980 --> 00:03:50,200
A few weeks later, the rock flies past

127
00:03:50,200 --> 00:03:50,480
Earth.

128
00:03:50,980 --> 00:03:52,820
Millions of people go outside to watch it

129
00:03:52,820 --> 00:03:53,680
cross the sky.

130
00:03:54,400 --> 00:03:56,600
Nia watches from above the crowd, where nobody

131
00:03:56,600 --> 00:03:57,080
looks.

132
00:03:57,620 --> 00:03:58,540
Bo carries the folding chairs.

133
00:03:59,080 --> 00:04:00,680
The news calls it a lucky miss.

134
00:04:01,520 --> 00:04:04,200
Nobody knows that five teenagers moved it.

135
00:04:04,880 --> 00:04:06,820
Kai finally opens the crisps.

136
00:04:07,840 --> 00:04:09,880
So which power would you bring to save

137
00:04:09,880 --> 00:04:10,240
the planet?

138
00:04:10,720 --> 00:04:11,340
Tell us in the comments.

139
00:04:11,960 --> 00:04:14,080
And subscribe, because the five of you are

140
00:04:14,080 --> 00:04:15,020
just getting started.
